186339122 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339123 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339124 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339125 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339126 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338903 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339063 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339066 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339069 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339070 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339071 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339072 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338896 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338897 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338898 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338893 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338894 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338895 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338797 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338979 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338889 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063714 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338595 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064948 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 6t + 6t^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x = 2$ when $t = 0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064948 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064948 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063744 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338531 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064953 1071 "Jason, a biologist, is measuring the growth of bacteria in a Petri dish by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of bacteria in the dish.\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bacteria in the dish is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$?" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064953 1071 "Jason, a biologist, is measuring the growth of bacteria in a Petri dish by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of bacteria in the dish.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bacteria in the dish is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)?$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064953 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of bacteria inside the dish after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bacteria is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${N}^{′}(t)=r(t).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063750 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338361 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064954 1071 "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = 9\\sin^{2} (x)$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064954 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{sin}^{2}⁡(x).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064954 1074 "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=9{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=18u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =18u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063756 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338404 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064955 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = t - 2t^{4}$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x = \\frac{1}{10}$ and velocity $v = \\frac{1}{10}$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186064955 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=t-2{t}^{4}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{10}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{10}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186064955 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}(1)^{6}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{15}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{10}+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339096 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339097 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339098 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339099 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339095 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339106 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339107 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339108 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339129 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338778 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338786 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338787 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339120 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338793 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338794 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339134 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339139 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068218 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186069513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338662 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069513 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0^{+})(\\ln (\\sin x))/(\\csc x)$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186069513 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069513 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068230 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186069515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338691 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069515 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186069515 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{3x^{2} - 1}{x^{3} - x}dx$?" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186069515 1074 "Let $u={x}^{3}-x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}-x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339100 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339101 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339103 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339104 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338772 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338774 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078495 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339102 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186078510 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339074 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338962 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082421 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338730 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083920 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-9{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083920 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 2t - 9t^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v =-2m/s$ when $t = 1s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t = 2s$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083920 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-9{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}+C \\\\ -2 & =1-3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{2}-3(2)^{3} \\\\  & =4-24 \\\\  & =-20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082457 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338395 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083926 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+7⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=4.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083926 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n$r = ⟨\\frac{t^{2}}{2}, 3t + 7⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t = 4$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083926 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+7)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=4,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nv=⟨4,3⟩\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=4$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(4)| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=4$ is $5.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082469 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338568 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083928 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = \\frac{1}{2}t^{4} - 3t^{3} + t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v =-10m/s$ when $t = 0s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t = 2s$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186083928 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-10\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186083928 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{1}{10}(0)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ -10 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-10.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{1}{10}(2)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =3.2-12+2-10 \\\\  & =-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $16.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338850 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338851 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338852 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338857 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338873 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338874 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339001 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339002 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339012 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339013 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339022 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339023 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339034 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339035 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339036 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338734 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338840 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338841 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338842 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339037 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339039 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339040 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186087741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339041 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [