426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088286 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 1}{9x + 8x^{2} - x^{3}}$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186088286 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088286 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086382 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186088293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338695 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088293 1071 "![](images/c5/c5d36b72e7e2ff6754152a559d64bf3610708c9a2d3790c859bc9abacc9220b6.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088293 1071 "![](images/c5/c5d36b72e7e2ff6754152a559d64bf3610708c9a2d3790c859bc9abacc9220b6.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186088293 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086388 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186088294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338614 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088294 1071 "Write the complex number $z =-1 + \\textrm{i}$ in exponential form.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186088294 1071 "Write the complex number $z=-1+\\text{i}$ in exponential form." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186088294 1074 "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338951 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338953 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338954 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339060 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339061 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339011 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338959 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338963 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339016 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339018 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339020 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339021 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339056 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339059 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339062 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091036 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186092811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338690 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186092811 1070 #ed