045585 true] [17592186129842 1071 "Given that\n$g(x)|= {5x - 5,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; x^{3} - 1, \\mid x > 1$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) = lim_(x → 1^{-})g(x)$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)g(x) = g(1)$\n3. $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x = 1$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129842 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129842 1074 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128509 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338477 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129843 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/d7/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128515 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338647 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129844 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/43/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128539 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338335 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129848 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles $x^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = 1$ and $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129848 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129848 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/24/24cbd9a0e01023dca6a289838d94efe3c7b67abe5b7a1f275f6945f280e65d64.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/6c/6c51abe5c21571a9c5596fce4e3b8ace210eacfab125016e6b9a3b2a7bc2e056.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/f9/f9fed5856eda128d84debec320bc38a35eaa2bf5184b1f81cb1194b02f8a306a.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128557 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338608 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129851 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,1].$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129851 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $∬_(R)\\sin xe^{y}dA$ over the rectangle $R = [0, π] \\cdot [0, 1]$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129851 1074 "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }({e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi }\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128587 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338433 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129856 1071 "${\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij=$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186129856 1071 "What is $∑_(j = 1)^(2)∑_(i = 1)^(3)ij$?" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186129856 1074 "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}(1j+2j+3j) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}(j+2j+3j) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}6j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}6j & =6\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186133771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338880 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338934 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338949 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338950 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338929 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338930 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338931 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338932 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338933 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338952 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338955 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338956 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186147433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338957 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146942 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338333 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148203 1071 "Given the two planes $3x - y + z = 0$ and $x - z + 4 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0$.\n2. The planes are parallel.\n3. The planes intersect on a line." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148203 1071 "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148203 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146948 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338327 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148204 1071 "Given the two planes $-x - 2y + z = 0$ and $2x + 4y - 2z + 1 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes are coincident." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148204 1071 "Given the two planes $-x-2y+z=0$ and $2x+4y-2z+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148204 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y-2z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z+1=0 \\\\ x+2y-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ -x-2y+z-\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146960 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338386 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148206 1071 "The intersection of the planes $-x + y - 2z = 0$ and $-x - 2y + z - 3 = 0$ is given by the line\n\n$r(t) = ⟨a_{1}, a_{2}, 0⟩ + tb, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\nCalculate $a_{1} + a_{2}$, and find a possible direction vector $b$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186148206 1071 "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186148206 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186171964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338781 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186171964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338782 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186171964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338783 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186171964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338784 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186171964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338785 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171213 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186172603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338326 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172603 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\frac{4}{2})$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186172603 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172603 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=4$ and $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}) & =\\frac{4!}{(4-2)!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2!}{2!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171219 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186172604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338694 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172604 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\frac{6}{3})$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186172604 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186172604 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=6$ and $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}) & =\\frac{6!}{(6-3)!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3!}{3!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot \\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3\\cdot 2}\\cdot \\cancel{3!}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339140 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338805 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338806 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338808 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338809 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338875 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338876 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338800 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338807 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338810 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175492 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186176564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338669 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176564 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176564 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^{3} - 6t^{2}, t \\ge 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186176564 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-12t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-12t) \\\\  & =6t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-12 \\\\ 6t & =12 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(2)=3(2)^{2}-12(2)=-12.\n$$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175498 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186176565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338481 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176565 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t > 0$ is given by $v(t) = 4t + 3t^{2}$. At time $t = 1$ the particle is located at the position $x = 12$. What is the position of the particle at time $t = 4$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186176565 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=4t+3{t}^{2}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=12.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186176565 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =12+{\\int }_{1}^{4}(4t+3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+[2{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12+([2(4)^{2}+{4}^{3}]-[2(1)^{2}+{1}^{3}]) \\\\  & =12+([32+64]-[2+1]) \\\\  & =12+(96-3) \\\\  & =12+93 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=105.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338761 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338779 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338780 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338788 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338789 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338790 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339130 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186179728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339131 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186184840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183619 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186184840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338331 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186184840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186184840 1071 "Find the solution of $-x^{2} + 3x \\ge 0$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186184840 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186184840 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ -x(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/68/68d82c96c820a6a0bf5e3c74ea93cbf2091e96ebfa7dfe200e8beb140cb7962e.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches the $x$-axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $0\\le x\\le 3.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338994 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338995 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338997 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338999 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339000 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339003 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339004 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339014 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186193383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192195 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186193383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338357 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186193383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186193383 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\,0<x<1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le {y}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186193383 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 < y < x^{2}, 0 < x < 1}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 1 \\le x \\le 4, 1 \\le y^{2} \\le 4}$\n3. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le x \\le 3,-1 \\le y \\le 1}$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186193383 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338860 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339026 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338881 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339032 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339038 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339028 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197834 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186198999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338375 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198999 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$ and the line\n${x = t; y = 2t,; z = t$ where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186198999 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186198999 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8(t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+4(t)^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-1$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ z=(-1)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-1,-2,-1).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(1)=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=(1)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1,2,1).$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186234321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338344 13194139533426 17592186045585 true] [17592186234321 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" 13194139533426 17592186045585 false] [17592186234321 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over